<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Creones</h1><p class="st-meta">100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">People of the west Highland coast of Scotland, named only in Ptolemy's 2nd-century Geography.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Creones are known solely from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Ptolemy's Geography</a> of about 150, which places them on the western seaboard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> north of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/epidii">Epidii</a>, usually located somewhere between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorn">Lorn</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardnamurchan">Ardnamurchan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knoydart">Knoydart</a>. Their name has no clear meaning, and some editors read it as a corrupted form.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nothing is recorded of their rulers or society. The rugged coast and sea lochs of this region were settled in small communities living in duns, crannogs and roundhouses, relying on fishing, herding and limited farming. Ptolemy's information probably came from sailors who passed along the coast, perhaps during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola's</a> fleet operations.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Creones are not mentioned again. Their region was later divided between the Gaelic kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> in the south and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> further north.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/creones.jpg" alt="Creones" width="320" height="464"><figcaption>Image: (myself) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britain.north.peoples.Ptolemy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic (Pictish)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>

